// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Puppet King
// @category Political / Power
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Puppet_King
// @domain   Political
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the nominal leader of a nation — most often royalty — is secretly a
// figurehead, while the real decisions are made by elements within the government (a Regent for
// Life, a Government Conspiracy, an Evil Chancellor — "The Man Behind the Man"). The crown holds
// the AUTHORITY; another holds the CONTROL. Distinct from its power-scale cousins by WHERE the will
// lives: the Rightful King Returns is a LEGITIMATE ruler reclaiming the throne; the Coup SEIZES the
// apparatus outright and the seizer becomes Ruler; the Decadent Court isolates the ruler from
// truth but leaves the will on the throne. The Puppet King leaves the title in place and moves the
// will OFF it — the figurehead reigns, the power-behind rules.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict, authority
import "trl/modules/power_dynamics.trl"    // Ruler, Authority, Commands — and the legitimacy it nominally wears

assoc puppet_king -> [
  power_dynamics : "the will-off-the-throne variant of",
  the_coup       : "a quieter cousin of",
  decadent_court : "the court that has captured the crown becomes a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// The figurehead bears the trappings of rule (Royalty, Authority, a Crown) but not the will of it;
// the trope NAMES the hollow between title and command.
imply PuppetKing -> [Royalty, Authority, Figurehead, Power, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: a crowned figurehead whose orders are issued by another — the power behind the
// throne commanding the one who wears it — IS a Puppet King. The discriminator binds on $puppet
// (the Commands subject): it must overtly be a [+Figurehead], not merely a [+Ruler], or the
// pattern would fire on any ruler who is ever given counsel.
rule Puppet_King {
  when:
    char $puppet [+Ruler] [+Figurehead]
    evt  $control [&Commands(agent=$power, subject=$puppet)]
    $power != $puppet                        // the will rests with ANOTHER, not the one on the throne
  then:
    $puppet [-Agency]                         // the figurehead reigns but does not decide
    $power [+PowerBehindThrone]
    $control [+PuppetKing]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: the rois fainéants — Childeric III & Pepin the Short (history, 8c) ===
// The last Merovingian "do-nothing kings" kept the long hair and the throne while the Mayors of
// the Palace ran Francia. Childeric III wore the crown; Pepin the Short gave the orders, mustered
// the armies, and kept the realm — until 751, when Pepin simply asked the Pope who SHOULD be king,
// got the answer he wanted, and shaved the figurehead's head into a monastery. Here, the years
// before the asking: the crown reigns, the Mayor rules.

// CHILDERIC III — the do-nothing king, crowned and hollow
char childeric [+Ruler] [+Royalty] [+Figurehead]

// PEPIN THE SHORT — Mayor of the Palace, the power behind the throne
char pepin [+Protagonist]

// THE PALACE AT COMPIÈGNE — where the orders are actually given
set compiegne [+Interior] [+Day]

scene the_mayors_palace {
  beat 1 {
    childeric @ compiegne
    pepin @ compiegne
    evt the_writ [&Commands(agent=pepin, subject=childeric)]   // the Mayor's word, in the King's name
    // Puppet_King -> childeric [-Agency], pepin [+PowerBehindThrone], the_writ [+PuppetKing]
  }
}
